# tools/ales_stereo_chorus_witness.rish — stereo_chorus: the triangle-LFO modulated delay mixed dry-plus-wet, carried into stereo, the same LFO and mix on both channels, each channel read off its own frozen snapshot — the fourth rung of the stereo modulation class and the vibrato's parent (ALES179).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES179.
# Design read: active-designing/20260815-083932_fill-ales179-lotus-stereo-chorus.md
# Stands on: lotus/chorus.rye (ALES71 — the mono chorus run once per channel, its ChorusError reused whole and its precheck (newly factored out) reused for the up-front atomic validation), lotus/vibrato.rye (ALES72 — the wet-only child),
# lotus/stereo_vibrato.rye (ALES178 — the stereo wet-only voice this parent adds under the dry, the vibrato-family equality target), lotus/glide.rye (ALES70 — glide_scale, 256 = unity), lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 — the StereoClip), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 — the Clip and the one true saturate).
#
# lotus/stereo_chorus.rye widens a stereo master: it runs ALES71's proven mono chorus on BOTH channels with the SAME LFO and mix — a triangle of depth round centre over period samples driving a modulated fractional delay, mixed under
# the dry at num/den. ALES178 carried the vibrato (the same delay heard wet-only) into stereo; the chorus is that delay mixed under the dry at a named level, so it returns the one fault the vibrato dropped — BadLevel (a zero
# denominator or a wet above unity) — and StereoChorusError carries five faults where the vibrato carried four. The same LFO and mix on both channels, each on its own snapshot, is the linked stereo chorus, both voices widening in
# lockstep. The LFO and mix are scaled units and fractions the caller names, not scalars measured across the field, so the same parameters on each channel preserve the image for free; mono chorus snapshots the dry prefix of its own
# clip, so run once per channel the left reads the left's dry and the right the right's, never crossed — no cross-channel snapshot. Unlike the wet-only vibrato, the write is saturate(dry + level times wet), so a loud dry-plus-wet sum
# clamps to the rail per channel; because the read and mix are linear in the input away from saturation, an identical-channel master stays identical and a panned master keeps its pan exactly. It validates the LFO, mix, and span once
# against the shared length before either channel is snapshotted (BadPeriod, BadDepth, BadDelay, BadLevel, BadRange), so a refusal never widens one channel and leaves the other torn; ChorusError reused whole. Values only, never a
# length; the two channels leave balanced. The crux is five laws: the stereo chorus law (each channel equal to ALES71's mono chorus with the same LFO and mix byte for byte, the channels genuinely differing), the vibrato-family law (a
# full-wet stereo chorus equal to the dry master plus ALES178's stereo vibrato per channel, sample for sample), the dry-identity law (a zero-level chorus the dry master on both), the image law (an identical-channel master staying
# identical, a panned pair keeping its ratio), and the balance / silence / atomicity / degenerate law.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_stereo_chorus_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-chorus: Language EN — stereo_chorus widens a stereo master by running ALES71's proven mono chorus on both channels with the same LFO and mix: a triangle of depth round centre over period samples driving a modulated fractional delay, mixed under the dry at num over den. ALES178 carried the vibrato, the same delay heard wet-only, into stereo; the chorus is that delay mixed under the dry at a named level, so it returns the one fault the vibrato dropped, BadLevel, a zero denominator or a wet above unity, and it carries five faults where the vibrato carried four. The same LFO and mix on both channels, each on its own snapshot, is the linked stereo chorus, both voices widening in lockstep. The LFO and mix are scaled units and fractions the caller names, 256 being unity, not scalars measured across the field, so the same parameters on each channel preserve the image for free; mono chorus snapshots the dry prefix of its own clip, so run once per channel the left reads the left's dry and the right the right's, never crossed, no cross-channel snapshot. Unlike the wet-only vibrato, the write is the saturate of dry plus level times wet, so a loud dry-plus-wet sum clamps to the rail per channel; because the read and mix are linear in the input away from saturation, an identical-channel master stays identical and a panned master keeps its pan exactly. It validates the LFO, mix, and span once against the shared length before either channel is snapshotted, so a refusal never widens one channel and leaves the other torn; it writes values only, never a length, so the two channels leave balanced."
say "ales-stereo-chorus: Style Radiant — the chorus is what a keeper actually reaches for: one voice made to sound like several, the width a mix wants on a thin vocal or a lone guitar. ALES178 carried its wet-only child the vibrato into stereo, the first delay-line stereo lift; the vibrato is exactly this chorus with the dry removed and the wet at full, so carrying the chorus itself into stereo is the natural parent rung, the same shared LFO on both channels each on its own snapshot but now the delayed voice mixed under the dry at a named level rather than heard alone. That mix returns the one fault the vibrato dropped, BadLevel, so the stereo chorus carries five faults where the vibrato carried four. This rung runs ALES71's proven chorus on each channel with the same LFO and mix, so both voices widen in lockstep and the image is held; each channel reads its own frozen snapshot, the left the left's dry and the right the right's, never crossed, so there is no cross-channel snapshot. Unlike the wet-only vibrato the write adds the dry under the wet and saturates once, so a loud sum clamps to the rail rather than wrapping; because the read and the mix are linear in the input away from saturation, an identical-channel master stays identical and a panned pair kept off the rail keeps its left to right ratio at every sample. The crux is five laws: the stereo chorus law, each channel equal to ALES71's mono chorus with the same LFO and mix byte for byte with the two channels genuinely differing so no channel is crossed; the vibrato-family law, a full-wet stereo chorus equal to the dry master plus ALES178's stereo vibrato per channel sample for sample, so the parent and the wet-only child are one delay heard two ways in stereo; the dry-identity law, a zero-level chorus equal to the dry master on both channels so the mix genuinely governs the wet; the image law, an identical-channel master staying identical and a panned pair keeping its left to right ratio; and the balance, silence, atomicity, and degenerate law, an all-silent master widening to silence on both with the lengths held, a bad period, a bad depth, a bad delay top, a bad level, and an out-of-range span each refusing by name with both channels untouched and balanced, and count zero the identity on both."
say "ales-stereo-chorus: Lens TAME — two bounded in-process i16 clips (left, right), each rung through ALES71's own modulated delay, the triangle and the fractional interpolation run in i64 and each channel snapshotting its own dry prefix into one bounded max_clip-wide i16 scratch, the dry-plus-wet sum saturating once; the two channels enter balanced (the StereoClip invariant asserted on entry) and leave balanced (a chorus writes values only, each length asserted equal and within bound on exit); the LFO, mix, and span are validated once against the shared length before either channel is snapshotted (ALES71's precheck, factored out of the mono chorus and shared, mirroring the ALES72 vibrato and ALES75 ring modulator refactors), so a refusal never widens one channel and leaves the other torn; StereoChorusError is ALES71's ChorusError (BadPeriod, BadDepth, BadDelay, BadLevel, BadRange) reused whole, the stereo lift adding no fault and BadLevel being the one the wet-only stereo vibrato dropped, returned by the mix. The stereo chorus law is proven by running both stereo_chorus and ALES71's mono chorus on the same per-channel content and demanding byte-for-byte equality on both channels while the two channels genuinely differ; the vibrato-family law proves a full-wet stereo chorus equals the dry master plus ALES178's stereo vibrato per channel sample for sample; the dry-identity law proves a zero-level chorus equals the dry master on both; the image law proves an identical-channel master stays identical and a right-is-half-left pan kept off the rail keeps its ratio sample for sample; and the balance, silence, atomicity, and degenerate cases prove an all-silent master staying silent on both, the length held, each of the five refusals leaving both channels untouched and balanced, and an empty span the identity on both; siloed to lotus/, the LFO in scaled units rather than hertz and the mix a named fraction rather than a decibel, no cross-channel snapshot, no feedback, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_chorus"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_chorus.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-chorus: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_chorus.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-chorus: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-chorus: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-chorus' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-chorus: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-chorus line"
